          "type": "text",
          "text": "I created `customers.json` from `customers.csv`. It's an array of 87 records in the original row order. I checked the first few records and the last one in the output, plus the raw bytes of the CSV.\n\n- **`id`:** kept as a string, so leading zeros stay (`\"00000\"`).\n- **`note`:** kept as a string. Commas, escaped quotes (`a \"quote\"`) and the embedded line breaks inside quoted fields all come through, so `\"two\\nlines\"` stays one value.\n- **`active`:** now `true` or `false`. The script would have stopped on any other value, and there were none.\n- **`age`:** blank becomes `null`; everything else is an integer.\n\nTwo things you might not expect:\n- The CSV uses Windows-style line endings (`\\r\\n`) between rows, but the line breaks inside notes are plain `\\n`. They stay as `\\n`.\n- Rows with no note (like `00003`) get `\"note\": \"\"`, not `null`, because you only asked for blank ages to become `null`.\n\nExample output:\n```json\n{\"id\": \"00000\", \"note\": \"olá, mundo\", \"active\": true, \"age\": null}\n```"
